sections.body:
Function calling (also called tool calling, or tool use) is the previous concept's schema-constrained generation, aimed at a specific and now-familiar shape: instead of a schema describing arbitrary extracted fields, each available tool comes with a schema describing its name and the exact arguments it accepts. You send the model a normal conversation plus a list of these tool definitions; at each turn, the model can either respond with ordinary text, or respond with a structured tool-call request -- a tool name plus a JSON object of arguments, generated under exactly the same kind of grammar constraint covered in the previous concept, so the arguments are guaranteed to be syntactically valid and to match the declared parameter schema.

```python
tools = [{
    "type": "function",
    "function": {
        "name": "get_order_status",
        "description": "Look up the current shipping status of a customer order by its order ID.",
        "parameters": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {"order_id": {"type": "string", "description": "The order ID, e.g. 'ORD-48213'."}},
            "required": ["order_id"]
        }
    }
}]

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="gpt-4.1", messages=messages, tools=tools
)
tool_call = response.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0]
# tool_call.function.name == "get_order_status"
# tool_call.function.arguments == '{"order_id": "ORD-48213"}'
```

Critically, receiving this response does not mean the order lookup has happened -- it means the model has generated a request that your code is now responsible for interpreting. Your application parses `tool_call.function.arguments`, decides whether to actually run the corresponding real function (call a database, hit an internal API, run a calculation), runs it, and then appends the real result as a new message in the conversation before calling the model again. Only at that point does the model see the actual answer and get to use it -- the loop, at minimum, is: send tools + conversation, receive a tool-call request, execute for real, append the real result, send again, receive a final text answer.

Schema-constrained arguments are guaranteed to be syntactically valid and correctly typed -- and, as the previous concept covered, guaranteed correctness of syntax is not the same thing as guaranteed correctness of content. A tool call requesting `cancel_subscription` with `{"user_id": "usr_4471"}` is perfectly well-formed JSON regardless of whether `usr_4471` is the actual user in this conversation, a different user entirely, or an ID the model fabricated because the conversation never explicitly stated one and a plausible-looking ID was the highest-probability continuation anyway. This is the exact same hallucination mechanism from the how-LLMs-work module, now applied to structured arguments instead of prose -- and it is, if anything, more dangerous here, precisely because the output looks so clean and machine-trustworthy that it's tempting to execute it without a second look.

```python
def execute_tool_call(tool_call, session):
    args = json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)
    if tool_call.function.name == "cancel_subscription":
        # NEVER trust args["user_id"] directly for a destructive action --
        # verify it against the actual authenticated session, not the model's claim.
        if args["user_id"] != session.authenticated_user_id:
            raise PermissionError("Tool call targets a user outside the current session.")
        return cancel_subscription(session.authenticated_user_id)
    raise ValueError(f"Unknown tool: {tool_call.function.name}")
```

The general principle: any argument that flows into a side-effecting or consequential action (money movement, account changes, deletions, sending communications) should be validated against ground truth your application already trusts -- session state, database lookups, business rules -- rather than executed on the strength of the model's say-so alone, no matter how well-formed the JSON looks. Read-only, low-stakes tools (a calculator, a unit converter) can reasonably tolerate looser validation than anything that changes real state.

